# Language

<!--
Purpose: the ubiquitous language of Gatekeeper Bastion. One name per thing, used in code,
in builder docs, in player docs and in the game's own text. This file is the authority.
Status: working
Update trigger: when a term is added, renamed, or retired — in the same commit as the rename.
Last updated: 2026-08-06
-->

**One thing, one name, everywhere.** The same word in the code, in `Docs/`, in
`Docs/Play/`, in the HUD, in a log line, and in anything a player ever reads. A concept
with two names is a concept nobody can search for, and it is how a codebase and its
documentation start describing two different games.

This is a standing rule, not a style preference. It applies to agents and to the developer
equally, and it applies the moment a name is chosen — renaming later costs a sweep of
every file above.

## The rules

**1. If it is in this table, use this word.** Not a synonym, not a shortened form, not a
more evocative one. The Hatch is not "the door", "the gate", "the objective" or "the
junction" in any sentence a player or an agent will read.

**2. If it is not in this table, do not coin one.** Inventing shorthand and then using it
as though it were agreed is the failure this file exists to stop. Say the plain thing:
*"the Primary's job is killing them before they reach the Hatch"* beats any abstraction
that compresses it. If a term genuinely earns its keep, it earns a row here first.

**3. Terms defined in `../../02_Systems.md` are usable in builder docs and never in player
docs.** `boarder-seconds`, `arrivals x dwell`, `realization` are analysis vocabulary. A
player has no way to look them up, so a page in `Docs/Play/` that uses one has failed.

**4. Code casing follows the code's own conventions; the *word* stays the same.**
`BoarderKind`, `RunPhase.Podded`, `MetaState.BankedSalvage` — the identifier can be
`PascalCase`, but it is never a different noun from the one below.

**5. A rename is one commit, everywhere.** Code, `Docs/`, `Docs/Play/`, and this table
together. A half-done rename is worse than the old name.

## The table

### The loop

| Term | Means | Never called |
|---|---|---|
| **Run** | One trip from the Ship to the Derelict and back, ending in a clear or a loss. | mission, level, raid, expedition |
| **the Ship** | The player's own vessel. The hub between Runs. | base, home, hub *(in player-facing text)* |
| **Derelict** | The wrecked vessel the player boards and defends inside. | station, ship *(ambiguous with the above)*, dungeon |
| **Hatch** | The umbilical junction back to the Ship. The defended objective. | door, gate, objective, junction |
| **embark** | Leaving the Ship for the Derelict, from the NAV MAP. | deploy, launch, start mission |
| **Prep** | The quiet state between Waves. Untimed, pays nothing, the only time the dial moves. | downtime, intermission, break |
| **Assault** | The state while a Wave is live. | combat, the fight |
| **the draw** | The player action that starts a Wave. | spawn, trigger, call the wave |
| **Wave** | One delivery of Boarders, started by one draw. | round, horde |
| **Podded** | The player is dead, or the Hatch fell. Both end the Run the same way. | death, game over, wipe, fail |
| **cleared** / **stripped** | Every Wave in the Run beaten. | won, completed, extracted |

### What comes at you

| Term | Means | Never called |
|---|---|---|
| **Boarder** | Any enemy. | enemy, mob, creature, hostile |
| **Default** | The Boarder that marches on the Hatch and can be provoked onto the player. | grunt, basic, marcher *(descriptive prose may say "marcher"; the kind's name is Default)* |
| **Hunter** | The Boarder that only ever wants the player. | chaser, stalker |
| **Ram** | The Boarder that only ever wants the Hatch and cannot be provoked. | brute, tank, charger |
| **breach point** | Where a group of Boarders enters the Derelict. | spawn point, portal |
| **group** | The unit Boarders arrive in — one breach point, several bodies, at once. | pack, squad, cluster |
| **provoke** | To make a Default target the player. Permanent for that Boarder's life. | aggro *(builder shorthand only, never player-facing)* |
| **shout** | A provoked Boarder pulling its neighbours onto the player. Does not relay. | alert, call, aggro chain |

### What you carry

| Term | Means | Never called |
|---|---|---|
| **Primary** | The earned weapon slot. Heavy damage. | main, rifle slot |
| **Secondary** | The starting weapon slot. Never runs dry. | sidearm slot *(the MK-1 is a sidearm; the slot is the Secondary)*, backup |
| **Utility** | The slot for thrown and placed items — mines, grenades, decoys, charges. | gadget, tool, equipment, melee *(the slot formerly named Melee)* |
| **Tier** | A step on a weapon's upgrade track. | level, rank, mark, upgrade |
| **the wheel** | The ARMORY's five-spoke upgrade selector. | carousel *(the shape is a wheel; the ring is undirected)*, tree |
| **spoke** | One of the wheel's five attributes. | node, slot, stat |
| **focus** | The spoke the player centres before buying. | specialisation, path, build *(a build is the result, not the act)* |
| **clip** | Rounds in the weapon now. | magazine, mag, ammo |

### The economy

| Term | Means | Never called |
|---|---|---|
| **Salvage** | The only currency. Banked the instant a Boarder dies. | credits, scrap, cash, resources |
| **banked** | Salvage the player holds and can spend. | stored, saved |
| **the siphon dial** | The per-Wave wager: more Salvage per Boarder, more Boarders. | difficulty, risk slider, multiplier |
| **track** | A permanent upgrade line at CREW QUARTERS or FABRICATION. | skill, perk, stat line |

### The stations

Written in caps in player-facing text, matching what the player reads on the console.

| Term | Owns | Never called |
|---|---|---|
| **ARMORY** | The weapon in your hands. | weapons bay, shop |
| **CREW QUARTERS** | The player's own attributes. | barracks, skills |
| **FABRICATION** | The Ship's gear and the Hatch. | engineering *(Engineering is a CREW QUARTERS attribute — the collision is real, do not blur it)*, workshop |
| **NAV MAP** | Where you go, and going there. | map, bridge, terminal |

## The trap in this table

**FABRICATION the station and Engineering the attribute are different things**, and the
station used to be called Engineering in the code — `EngineeringStation.cs` still is, and
the class comment says so on purpose. One is a console that sells Hatch gear; the other is
a personal attribute that multiplies how fast the player repairs. Any sentence that could
mean either is wrong and must be rewritten.

## Adding a term

A term earns a row when it names something a player or an agent will need to refer to more
than once. Add the row in the same commit that introduces the thing. If you find yourself
about to write a definition in a milestone or a wiki page, the definition belongs here and
the page links to it.
